{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Refined Prototype and Capstone Defense","title":"Persona-Based Simulation Checks","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting persona-based simulation checks to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_refined_prototype_and_capstone_defense","ail606_supplement_persona_based_simulation_checks","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":3,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"06","section":"03","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/06/02"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_6_3_persona_based_simulation_checks_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_6_3_persona_based_simulation_checks_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_6_3_persona_based_simulation_checks_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_6_3_persona_based_simulation_checks_calibration"]},"content":"# Persona-Based Simulation Checks\n\n![Persona-Based Simulation Checks textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/06-03-persona-based-simulation-checks.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nYou cannot recruit every learner who will use your prototype, but you can simulate the ones most likely to fail. A persona-based simulation check is a structured walkthrough in which you adopt a specific learner's goals, constraints, and context, then drive your prototype the way that learner actually would. Done well, it surfaces failures before real learners hit them; done poorly, it confirms only that a confident, well-resourced user on the happy path succeeds, which you already knew. The technique comes from usability engineering's cognitive walkthrough, where an analyst steps through a task asking at each point whether the user would know what to do and whether the system would tell them they succeeded.\n\nThe reason this belongs in a capstone defense is coverage. A committee will ask \"who did you test this for,\" and a real persona check lets you answer with the constraints you deliberately stressed (low bandwidth, audio off, screen reader, time pressure) rather than with a demographic sketch that proves nothing.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student writes: \"Persona: Maria, 42, marketing director. She completed the module with no problems.\" This check found nothing because nothing in it could fail. \"Marketing director, 42\" is a demographic label, not a set of constraints, and \"completed the module\" means the student walked the happy path. The persona never encountered a condition the design did not already handle.\n\nNow imagine the student instead drove a persona defined by constraints: finishing required training on a phone, on a commute, with intermittent signal and audio off. Suddenly the check has friction. The signal drops mid-video; the module resets; the audio-only narration is inaccessible muted. None of these are demographic facts; they are conditions of use, and conditions of use are what break designs. This section is about building personas that can fail.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA persona-based simulation check is a **constraint-driven walkthrough**, and its diagnostic power comes entirely from how the persona is constructed. A persona that exists to break the design has three load-bearing parts, none of which is demographic:\n\n- **Goal:** the specific outcome the learner is trying to reach, which lets you judge whether each screen advances or blocks it.\n- **Constraint:** the limiting condition (device, bandwidth, sensory channel, time, prior knowledge) that the design must survive. This is the part that produces failures.\n- **Context of use:** where and how the interaction actually happens, which determines whether your assumptions about attention and environment hold.\n\nThese map onto two course anchors. UDL's principle of learner variability supplies the constraints worth simulating: variability in perception (audio off, screen reader), in physical access (one-handed phone use), and in executive function (interruption, time pressure). Cognitive load theory supplies the failure mechanism: when a constrained learner loses a channel (muted audio) or context (a reset), the design may force them to reconstruct state from working memory, which is exactly the extraneous load that causes drop-off.\n\nA constraint-driven walkthrough runs in a fixed order: build the persona from its three load-bearing parts before you touch the prototype, then drive it into friction, then convert what breaks into a revision. Put the steps in sequence.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Define the goal: the specific outcome the learner is trying to reach\", \"Add the constraint: the limiting condition the design must survive (device, channel, time)\", \"Set the context of use: where and how the interaction actually happens\", \"Drive the persona off the happy path until a constraint collides with a design assumption\", \"Convert the observed failure into a specific screen revision (or a documented decision to accept it)\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\nThe critical move is to drive the persona off the happy path on purpose. A walkthrough that only confirms success has no evidential value, because a design that works for an unconstrained user on the intended path is the null hypothesis. The check earns its place only when the persona's constraint collides with a design assumption and produces a finding you can convert into a specific screen revision.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nHere is one persona-based simulation check written two ways. Read the before version, then the annotated after version.\n\n**Before (weak check):**\n\n> Persona: Maria, 42, marketing director. Walkthrough: completed the module. No problems found.\n\nThat check found nothing because the persona was a demographic label and the walkthrough never left the happy path. Now the same check rebuilt to do work:\n\n**After (annotated simulation):**\n\n1. **Persona (goal + constraint + context):** Devin must finish required onboarding on a phone during a 10-minute commute, with intermittent signal and audio off.\n2. **Path driven:** Started Frame 2, lost signal mid-video, returned to find the module had reset to Frame 1.\n3. **Failure observed:** No progress save and audio-only narration on Frame 2, so the segment was inaccessible muted.\n4. **Design consequence:** Added per-frame progress persistence and on-screen captions for all narrated frames. Logged this as a revision to screens 2 and 4.\n5. **Rejected alternative:** Considered a \"do not use on mobile\" warning instead. Rejected because the persona's context is the actual use context, not an edge case to exclude.\n\nThe after version names a goal, a constraint, and a context, drives the persona into friction, and converts the finding into a specific screen revision a committee can audit.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A classmate's persona reads: \\\"Devin, 35, enjoys hiking and podcasts, prefers dark mode.\\\" Why will this persona find nothing?\" explanation=\"Hobbies, age, and aesthetic preferences are demographic and stylistic filler. None of them is a goal, a constraint, or a context of use, so nothing in the persona can collide with a design assumption and produce a failure.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It carries no limiting constraint (device, channel, time, prior knowledge), so it cannot fail\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It is too short to be a real persona\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It names a context of use but no goal\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It will find too many failures to triage\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore you trust a simulation check, confirm it was capable of failing. A check that could only succeed tells you nothing about robustness.\n\n1. **Constraint test:** Does the persona carry a real limiting condition (device, channel, time, prior knowledge), not just an age and job title?\n2. **Off-path test:** Did you drive the persona into at least one condition the design did not obviously anticipate?\n3. **Conversion test:** Did each failure become a specific screen revision, or a documented decision to accept the limitation with a reason?\n\nIf the check produced no friction and no revision, it has not yet done diagnostic work; redefine the persona around a sharper constraint and run it again.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is mistaking a richer persona biography for a more useful persona. Backstory (hobbies, personality, a stock photo) adds nothing to a simulation; only goals, constraints, and context generate findings. A two-line persona with a sharp constraint outperforms a half-page profile with none.\n\nWhen a constraint-driven persona breaks your module, the next choice separates a robustness fix from a quiet narrowing of your audience. Walk the decision.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The mobile, audio-off persona just broke Frame 2\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Devin lost signal on a commute; the module reset to Frame 1 and the audio-only narration on Frame 2 was inaccessible muted. What is your first move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Add a \\\"best viewed on desktop with sound\\\" warning\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"This converts the design limitation into the learner problem. Devin commuting on a muted phone is the real use context, not an edge case you can warn away.\"},{\"label\":\"Add per-frame progress persistence and on-screen captions for narrated frames\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. You designed for the failing context, fixing the two assumptions (no save state, audio-only) the constraint exposed.\"},{\"label\":\"Mark the walkthrough \\\"no problems, completes on desktop\\\" and move on\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"A check that only succeeds on the happy path has no evidential value. You found a real failure; reporting none discards it.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"The warning ships. Devin still has to complete required onboarding on the only device available. What happened to the failure?\",\"feedback\":\"It did not get fixed; it got reassigned to the learner. The audience narrowed to exclude the actual use context, which a committee will read as avoiding the finding.\",\"choices\":[]},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"You re-run the same persona under the same constraint after the fix. What confirms the revision worked?\",\"feedback\":\"The persona that broke earlier now reaches the goal: state persists across the signal drop and captions make Frame 2 perceivable muted. The failure is gone, and you can log it as a revision to screens 2 and 4.\",\"choices\":[]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"You reported no problems. The real learners hit the reset and the muted narration in week one. What did the weak check cost?\",\"feedback\":\"It pushed a known, reproducible failure into production. The persona existed precisely to catch this before real learners did.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\nA second error is excluding the failing context instead of designing for it. When a mobile, audio-off persona breaks the module, the weak response is a \"best viewed on desktop\" warning. That converts the design's limitation into the learner's problem. The persona's context is the real use context, so the finding is a mandate to revise, not a license to narrow the audience.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nChoose the persona most likely to expose your weakest assumption. If your storyboard assumes attentive desktop viewing, build a persona who is mobile, interrupted, and muted, because that is where your assumptions are thinnest. Write only the goal, the constraint, and the context, then put the prototype in front of yourself and act as that learner, narrating every point where you hesitate, lose state, or cannot perceive content.\n\nWhen you hit the first failure, stop and record three things: what you were trying to do, what the design did instead, and which constraint caused the gap. Then design the fix at the screen level (progress persistence, captions, a larger touch target) and re-run the same persona to confirm the failure is gone. The walkthrough is complete when the persona that broke the design earlier now reaches the goal under the same constraint.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, draft the single persona whose constraint your current prototype is least prepared for, then predict where it will fail before you run it. Strong reading here is predictive: you should be able to name the screen that will break and the constraint that will break it, then use the walkthrough to confirm or correct the prediction.\n\nA strong reader of persona-based simulation checks can do four things:\n\n1. Build a persona from goal, constraint, and context with no demographic filler.\n2. Predict which screen a given constraint will stress.\n3. Convert an observed failure into a specific, inspectable revision.\n4. Justify designing for a failing context rather than excluding it.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong simulation check is judged by whether it found something. A walkthrough that reports \"no problems\" is either a sign of a flawless design (rare) or, far more often, a sign of a persona too weak to fail. The standard is met when the check documents a constraint-driven failure and the screen revision that resolved it, with a rejected alternative showing you chose to fix rather than to exclude. When you cannot tell whether a persona is strong enough, annotate it and request a comparison between a constraint-driven persona and a demographic one.\n\nA final paragraph for the artifact would read like this: \"I drove a constrained persona off the happy path, observed a failure caused by that constraint, and revised the specific screens responsible. I rejected the option of warning the learner away from their real context, because the context was the use case, not an edge case.\"\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_persona_based_simulation_checks\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\", \"ail606_supplement_refined_prototype_and_capstone_defense\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the technique to a domain where the constraint is cognitive rather than technical, for example a novice learner with no prior schema for the content. The walkthrough no longer stresses bandwidth or audio; it stresses prior knowledge. You drive a persona who cannot infer the unstated step, and you watch for the moment they stall because the design assumed expertise they lack. The skill that transfers is identifying which constraint matters most for this audience and building the persona around it, rather than reusing the mobile-and-muted persona that mattered for a different design.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/06-03-persona-based-simulation-checks-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"06.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **Mayer multimedia learning**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 06.03. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to cognitive load theory. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_persona_based_simulation_checks\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure each persona is built from goal, constraint, and context, that you drove it off the happy path, and that every failure became a specific revision. A check that could not fail is not yet evidence.\n\n## References\n\n- Mayer multimedia learning. https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_3_persona_based_simulation_checks_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_persona_based_simulation_checks","stem":"Which persona is most useful for stress-testing an onboarding module prototype?","options":["Maria, 42, marketing director.","A typical adult learner who is fairly motivated.","Devin, who must complete onboarding on a phone during a 10-minute bus commute with intermittent signal and no audio.","A power user who already knows the content."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"A simulation-useful persona names a goal, a constraint, and a context of use that can actually expose a design failure; the others are demographic labels.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_6_3_persona_as_demographic_label","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_persona_based_simulation_checks"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_3_persona_based_simulation_checks_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_persona_based_simulation_checks","stem":"You ran four personas through your prototype and every walkthrough finished successfully on the first attempt. What does this most likely indicate?","options":["The prototype is finished and needs no further checks.","You drove every persona down the happy path and never tested an interruption, error, or unsupported branch.","The personas were too difficult.","Four personas is too many to be valid."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A simulation in which nothing ever fails has not stressed the design; you must drive a persona into friction such as an interruption or low-bandwidth condition.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_6_3_happy_path_only_walkthrough","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_persona_based_simulation_checks"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_3_persona_based_simulation_checks_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"You prompt an AI to role-play a low-vision persona and it reports that your color-only status indicators are confusing. How should this appear in your defense?","options":["As a confirmed usability finding from a user with low vision.","As proof that the design is accessible because the AI completed the task.","As a candidate failure mode to verify against an accessibility guideline and, where possible, a real user, not as user data.","It should be omitted because AI cannot have a disability."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"An AI persona generates hypotheses to check; its reactions are not user data and must be verified against design criteria or a real user.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_6_3_ai_persona_as_real_user","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_3_persona_based_simulation_checks_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"Your simulation notes list six persona findings, but a reviewer says the check 'did no work.' The prototype is identical to before the simulation. What is the missing link?","options":["More personas should have been simulated.","Each finding needs to be paired with a specific screen or step you revised, or a justified decision not to.","The findings should be ranked alphabetically.","The simulation should be repeated until no findings appear."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A persona check earns credit only when findings become design consequences: a named revision or a justified decision to leave the design as is.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_6_3_finding_without_design_consequence","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision"]}]}}